# One line ending, everywhere.
#
# This repo is worked on from Windows with git's autocrlf on, so the same
# file was LF in one working copy and CRLF in the next. That is not cosmetic
# here — it broke work four times in three days, each time in a way that
# looked like something else:
#
#   A test matched a sentence across a line break, passed on the branch it
#   was written on, and failed the moment `master` was checked out. Nothing
#   about the code had changed; git had rewritten the file.
#
#   Three separate "break the test on purpose" mutations silently matched
#   nothing and reported NO-OP — which on a results line reads exactly like
#   a test that did its job.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises to LF in the index and checks out LF, so a
# working copy and a blob agree. Binary files are excluded below so they are
# never touched.
#
# After adding this, renormalise once:
#
#   git add --renormalize .
#   git commit -m "Normalise line endings"

* text=auto eol=lf

# Binaries — never touched.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.pdf  binary
*.docx binary
*.xlsx binary
